PRACTICES=[
('Ānāpānasati (breath meditation, "Method 2")','mun-boowa-line','Sit upright and notice the breath at whatever point it is clearest. Let it settle into a comfortable rhythm, then let that sense of ease spread through the whole body, using the breath energy as a home base for steady, alert attention.','https://www.dhammatalks.org/books/WithEachAndEveryBreath/Contents.html'),
('"Buddho" recollection','thai-forest','Silently repeat "bud-" on the in-breath and "-dho" on the out-breath to anchor the mind; when the mind is calm, drop the word and stay with the knowing itself. Used widely in the Ajahn Mun and Ajahn Chah lines.','https://www.accesstoinsight.org/lib/thai/chah/index.html'),
('Walking meditation (jongrom)','chah-line','Choose a straight path of 15–30 paces. Walk at a natural or slow pace, attention on the feet or the whole body; at each end, stop, turn, and re-collect before walking back.','https://www.accesstoinsight.org/lib/thai/chah/index.html'),
('Mettā (loving-kindness)','early-suttas','Wish well to yourself, then a benefactor, a friend, a neutral person, a difficult person and finally all beings, using simple phrases or a felt sense of goodwill, letting it spread in every direction.','https://www.accesstoinsight.org/tipitaka/kn/snp/snp.1.08.amar.html'),
('Mahasi noting','mahasi','Watch the rise and fall of the abdomen and give each event a soft mental label ("rising", "falling", "thinking", "hearing"), then return to the primary object; move to slow walking with labels for lifting, moving and placing.','https://www.accesstoinsight.org/lib/authors/mahasi/wheel370.html'),
('Body sweeping (Goenka vipassanā)','goenka-vipassana','After a few days of ānāpāna to sharpen attention, move attention systematically through the body from head to feet, observing every sensation with equanimity, as taught in ten-day residential courses.','https://www.dhamma.org/en/about/vipassana'),
('Natural awareness (right attitude)','shwe-oo-min','Instead of forcing focus on one object, relax and know whatever is being experienced, checking the attitude of the observing mind (craving, aversion, impatience) throughout daily activities.','https://ashintejaniya.org/teachings'),
('Satipaṭṭhāna (four establishings of mindfulness)','early-suttas','Contemplate body, feeling, mind and mental qualities as they arise and pass, ardent, clearly knowing and mindful, without greed or distress about the world (MN 10).','https://www.accesstoinsight.org/tipitaka/mn/mn.010.than.html'),
('RAIN','insight','Recognise what is happening; Allow it to be there; Investigate with kindness how it feels in the body; Nurture with self-compassion; then rest in the awareness that remains.','https://www.tarabrach.com/rain/'),
('Śravaṇa–manana–nididhyāsana','arsha-vidya','Systematic listening to the Upaniṣads with a teacher (śravaṇa), resolving doubts by reflection (manana), and dwelling on the understood meaning until it is assimilated (nididhyāsana).','https://arshabodha.org'),
('Japa (repetition of a divine name)','ramakrishna-order','Repeat a mantra or name of God given by the teacher, often with a mālā of 108 beads, letting the mind rest in its meaning and the feeling of devotion.','https://www.ramakrishnavivekananda.info/gospel/gospel.htm'),
('Hare Krishna mahāmantra (japa and kīrtan)','gaudiya','Chant "Hare Kṛṣṇa Hare Kṛṣṇa, Kṛṣṇa Kṛṣṇa Hare Hare / Hare Rāma Hare Rāma, Rāma Rāma Hare Hare" softly on beads (japa) or sing it together (kīrtan), as practised since Chaitanya.','https://vedabase.io/en/library/bg/'),
('Yoga nidrā','bihar-yoga','Lying down, rotate awareness through the body, then breath, opposite sensations and visualisations while staying awake, ending with a personal resolve (saṅkalpa).','https://www.biharyoga.net/'),
('Five points of yoga (Sivananda)','sivananda-yoga','Proper exercise (āsana), proper breathing (prāṇāyāma), proper relaxation, proper diet and positive thinking with meditation, as summarised by Swami Vishnudevananda.','https://www.sivananda.org/teachings/'),
('Breath-centred, individually adapted āsana (viniyoga)','krishnamacharya-yoga','Link every movement to a slow inhale or exhale and adapt sequence, length and intensity to the person\'s body, age and aims, as taught by Krishnamacharya and T. K. V. Desikachar.','https://www.kym.org/'),
]
